\begin_layout Title
Set 5 - Differences-in-Differences and Panel Data Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
A Formal Discussion of Diff-in-Diff
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Differences-in-differences is usually motivated by using time series variation
 in which we observe individuals/groups pre and post treatment.
 We could estimate
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=\alpha+\beta\mbox{Treated}_{t}+\epsilon_{it}\equiv\alpha+\beta\mbox{Post}_{t}+\epsilon_{it}
\]

\end_inset

Yet, do we believe 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(\mbox{Treated}_{t},\epsilon_{it})=0$
\end_inset

? Usually not because other determinants of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}$
\end_inset

 are likely changing as well.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What does 
\begin_inset Formula $E[y_{it}]$
\end_inset

 look like historically?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Is it flat? This assumption is more credible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Is there smooth trending? A model controlling for trend might also be credible.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Yet, using time series variation implies that treatment is the only reason
 outcomes differ systematically from pre to post.
 Making this case would be a tough sell.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is easier to use time series variation with high-frequency data.
 Then, we can compare outcomes 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

seconds
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 before and after treatment, such that other determinants are unlikely to
 have changed.
 This amounts to a regression discontinuity (RD) design with time as a running
 variable, but we'll come back to this later.
 However, this type of analysis is not useful if treatment is not expected
 to have an immediate effect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Differences-in-Differences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Difference-in-differences (diff-in-diff) brings an untreated comparison
 group to tell us how 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 would have envolved in the absence of treatment.
 With diff-in-diff, we compare changes in outcomes among a treated group
 to changes among an untreated (control) group.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Identifying Assumption:
\series default
\emph default
 changes for the control group provide a good counterfactural for changes
 that would have been observed for the treated group in the absence of treatment
, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[\Delta y_{i}^{0}|\mbox{G}_{i}=0]=E[\Delta y_{i}^{1}|\mbox{G}_{i}=0]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[\Delta y_{i}^{1}|\mbox{G}_{i}=1]-E[\Delta y_{i}^{0}|\mbox{G}_{i}=0]=E[\Delta y_{i}^{1}|\mbox{G}_{i}=1]-E[\Delta y_{i}^{1}|\mbox{G}_{i}=0]=\mbox{ATET}
\]

\end_inset

Note that we have just redefined the outcome and assumed
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta y^{0}\perp\mbox{G}
\]

\end_inset

If we went further and assumed that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta y^{1},\Delta y^{0}\perp\mbox{G}
\]

\end_inset

then we would have obtain the ATE.
 Then, the sample analogue of the diff-in-diff estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\theta}_{DD}=\left(\bar{y}_{t=1}^{\mbox{G}=1}-\bar{y}_{t=0}^{\mbox{G}=1}\right)-\left(\bar{y}_{t=1}^{\mbox{G}=0}-\bar{y}_{t=0}^{\mbox{G}=0}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Regression Analogue
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The regression analogue of diff-in-diff is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=\alpha+\beta\mbox{Post}_{t}+\gamma\mbox{TreatmentGroup}_{i}+\theta\mbox{Post}_{t}\times\mbox{TreatmentGroup}_{i}+\epsilon_{it}
\]

\end_inset

or written more compactly
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=\alpha+\beta\mbox{P}_{t}+\gamma\mbox{TG}_{i}+\theta(\mbox{P}_{t}\times\mbox{TG}_{i})+\epsilon_{it}
\]

\end_inset

Here are the descriptions of each coefficient:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is the change in the control group from the pre-period to the post-period
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 is the change from the control group to the treatment group in the pre-period.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is going to give us the diff-in-diff estimate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Remember, according to the identifying assumption, the differences between
 the treatment and the control group in the pre-period would have remained
 the same in the post-period in the absence of treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, consider the expected outcomes for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{TG}_{i}=1$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}^{1}=\alpha+\gamma+(\beta+\theta)\mbox{P}_{t}+\epsilon_{it}^{1}
\]

\end_inset

In the pre-period, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{P}_{t}=0$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i0}^{1}=\alpha+\gamma+\epsilon_{i0}^{1}
\]

\end_inset

In the post-period, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{P}_{t}=0$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i1}^{1}=\alpha+\gamma+(\beta+\theta)+\epsilon_{i1}^{1}
\]

\end_inset

The difference
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\beta+\theta+\epsilon_{i1}^{1}-\epsilon_{i0}^{1}
\]

\end_inset

captures the effect of treatment 
\begin_inset Formula $(\theta)$
\end_inset

 along with other differences between the pre and post periods 
\begin_inset Formula $(\beta+\epsilon_{i1}^{1}-\epsilon_{i0}^{1})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, consider the expected outcomes for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{TG}_{i}=0$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}^{0}=\alpha+\beta\mbox{P}_{t}+\epsilon_{it}
\]

\end_inset

In the pre-period, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{P}_{t}=0$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i0}^{0}=\alpha+\epsilon_{i0}^{0}
\]

\end_inset

In the post-period, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{P}_{t}=1$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i1}^{0}=\alpha+\beta+\epsilon_{i1}^{0}
\]

\end_inset

The difference
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\beta+\epsilon_{i1}^{0}-\epsilon_{i0}^{0}
\]

\end_inset

captures the difference between the pre and post periods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Differences-in-Differences Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As before, the diff-in-diff estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\theta}_{DD} & = & \left(\bar{y}_{1}^{1}-\bar{y}_{0}^{1}\right)-\left(\bar{y}_{1}^{0}-\bar{y}_{0}^{0}\right)\\
\hat{\theta}_{DD} & \begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array} & \theta+E\left[\left(\epsilon_{i1}^{1}-\epsilon_{i0}^{1}\right)-\left(\epsilon_{i1}^{0}-\epsilon_{i0}^{0}\right)\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

From the identifying assumption, the expected change over time for the untreated
 group
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[\beta+\epsilon_{i1}^{0}-\epsilon_{i0}^{0}]
\]

\end_inset

provides a good counterfactual for the expected change for the treated group
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[\beta+\theta+\epsilon_{i1}^{1}-\epsilon_{i0}^{1}]
\]

\end_inset

in the absence of treatment.
 This imples that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left[\epsilon_{i1}^{1}-\epsilon_{i0}^{1}\right]=E\left[\epsilon_{i1}^{0}-\epsilon_{i0}^{0}\right]
\]

\end_inset

which is often referred to as the 
\series bold
\emph on
common trends assumption
\series default
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
What Does Diff-in-Diff Provide?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Unlike differences across time, it is permissible if other (observed or
 unobserved) factors lead to changes in outcomes, so long as they similarly
 affect both the treated and the untreated groups.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Unlike differences across groups, it is permissible if there are (observed
 or unobserved) differences between groups related to outcomes, so long
 as they affect outcomes similarly in the pre and post periods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is evident from the regression equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=\alpha+\beta\mbox{P}_{t}+\gamma\mbox{TG}_{i}+\theta(\mbox{P}_{t}\times\mbox{TG}_{i})+\epsilon_{it}
\]

\end_inset

that we are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
controlling for systemic differences between the pre and post period that
 are common to both groups, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\mbox{P}_{t}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
controlling for systematic differences between the treatment and control
 groups that are constant over time, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\mbox{G}_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Benefits of the Regression Version
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It is convenient, especially for standard errors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We can control for time varying covariates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It easily extends to considering multiple treatment and control groups over
 multiple periods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We can consider treatment that happens at different times.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We can go beyond treatment to consider treatment intensity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $y_{igt}=\alpha_{g}+\alpha_{t}+\theta\mbox{Treated}_{igt}+\epsilon_{igt}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We can control for systematic differences across periods with time-period
 fixed effects.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We can control for systematic differences across groups with group fixed
 effects.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
As usual, the estimated effect is on the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

treated
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (i.e., the treatment group following treatment)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection*
Diff-in-Diff With Multiple Groups
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Do we care how many groups we have or do we simply care about the total
 number of individuals? Well, it depends on the strcutrue of the error terms.
 To follow up, if we think that errors are correlated within groups, then
 it becomes critical that we cluster our stadard errors so that we have
 a large number of clusters.
 If the errors are i.i.d., then all observations provide equal information
 regardless of which cluster they come from.
 Thus, it does not matter how many clusters we have.
 A discussion concerning the importance of clustered standard errors is
 still to come.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Finding Applications for Diff-in-Diff
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

After learning about some policy change and/or intervention, we usually
 employ a diff-in-diff strategy to analyze the resulting effects.
 The difficult procedure is to find a 
\bar under
valid
\bar default
 untreated comparison group.
 In fact, can we even demonstrate that the common trends assumption is reasonabl
e? In order to do so, we need to present a rhetorical and data driven case.
 Usually, we don't just do a data search for groups with common trends.
 Yet, within the appropriate context, selecting on common trends seems reasonabl
e, i.e., a synthetic control design.
\end_layout

