\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Remember, diff-in-diff implies an additive structure for potential outcomes,
 i.e., outcomes are determined by time-invariant state effects and year effects
 common to all states, plus treatment.
 So, we can write the model as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{st}=\beta_{NJ}+\beta_{PA}+\gamma_{Pre}+\gamma_{Post}+\theta\mbox{Treat}_{st}+\epsilon_{st}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The difference between NJ and PA in the pre-period: 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{NJ}-\beta_{PA}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The difference overtime for PA estiamtes: 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{Post}-\gamma_{Pre}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The difference overtime for NJ estimates: 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta+(\gamma_{Post}-\gamma_{Pre})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The diff-in-diff estimate: 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

On the other hand, a fully saturated model only has four parameters.
 We estimate
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{st}=\alpha+\beta NJ_{s}+\gamma Post_{t}+\theta(NJ_{s}\times Post_{t})+\epsilon_{st}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Expected 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 for PA in the pre-period: 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=\beta_{PA}+\gamma_{Pre}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Expected difference over time for PA: 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=\gamma_{Post}-\gamma_{Pre}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Expected difference between NJ and PA in the pre-period: 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=\beta_{NJ}-\beta_{PA}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Expected difference over time for NJ relative to PA: 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Earned Income Tax Credit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Question:
\series default
 How does generosity affect labor supply?
