\begin_layout Section*
Common Threats to Diff-in-Diff
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Different Trends
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
If the treatment group has a steeper positive trend than the control group,
 then the estimated effects are positively biased.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If the treatment group has a flatter positive trend than the control group,
 then the estimated effects are negatively biased.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Asherfelter's Dip
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
A situation in which selection into treatment is induced by a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bad shock
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to the outcome.
 This leads the estimated treatment effect to reflect both the treatment
 effect and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

getting back to normal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 shocks.
 Thus, the estimated treatment effect is positively biased.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Idiosyncratic Shocks
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Are any other 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

treatments
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 specific to the treatment or the control group?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Compositional changes caused by treatment
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Example*
People move into the treatment group because it is desirable.
 Then, the change over time for the treatment group reflects the effect
 of treatment, the effect of the composition change, and the effect of common
 shocks.
 The change over time for the control group reflects the effect of common
 shocks plus the effect of the composition change due to out migration (i.e.,
 people moving from the control group to the treatment group).
 Subtracting off the change of the control group from the change of the
 treatment group does not get rid of the composition effect, it potentially
 makes it worse.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
One Solution: 
\series default
Use individual level data to estimate an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

intent-to-treat
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 effect, i.e., estimate effects based on group status when treatment was assigned
 or occurred.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Section*
Extensions of Diff-in-Diff
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Diff-in-Diff With Dynamic Treatment Effects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Instead of having a 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Post}\times\mbox{Treatment}$
\end_inset

 dummy, we have a set of dummies indicating years leading/lagging treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
2 years pre-treatment, 1 year pre-treatment, ..., 4 years post-treatment, 5
 or more years post-treatment, etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The coefficient estimates on the dummies indicating pre-treatment years
 should be zero, which is a robustness check.
 The coefficient estimates on the dummies indicating post-treatment years
 will tell us how the treatment effect varies over time.
 With an event study, it is better to utilize a diff-in-diff model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is important to note that the set of treatment indicators give us the
 difference in outcomes relative to omitted leads/lags (controlling for
 expected changes due to the passage of time, i.e., year effects).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Relative to 3 years or more pre-treatment, if the last indicator was 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

5 years post-treatment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 instead of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

5 or more years post-treatment,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 then the difference would be relative to 3 or more years pre-treatment
 and 6 or more years post-treatment.
 This is not appropriate if we think 6 or more years are treated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When utilizing dynamic treatment effects, additional care is needed when:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Treatment happens at different times
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The panel is not balanced
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If either of these situations arise, then all treated individual/states/etc.
 may not contribute to estimating all lags.
 Consequently, what seems to be dynamic treatment effects may actually reflect
 heterogenous treatment effects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Texas was treated 2 years prior to the end of the panel, while other states
 are treated earlier.
 Then,
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Texas only contributes to estimates of short-run effects (1 and 2 years
 post-treatment).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If the effect for Texas is different from other states, then the estimate
 will change as we move from 2 to 3 years post-treatment, even if the true
 treatment effect is time-invariant.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
To overcome these problems, it is common to restrict the sample, or the
 analysis, so that the same groups contribute to all estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Allowing for Differential Trends
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This method allows the treatment and the control groups to have different
 trends.
 Thus, we are relaxing the common trends assumption.
 Instead, we are assuming that common shocks affect the trends of both groups
 in a similar manner.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For example,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{st}=\beta_{s}+\psi_{s}t+\gamma_{t}+\theta\mbox{Treated}_{st}+\epsilon_{st}
\]

\end_inset

The identification of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is based on comparing how outcomes diverge from trend for the treatment
 group following treatment, relative to how outcomes diverge from trend
 for the control group.
 This method requires three or more years of data, but this is not a serious
 restriction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assuming constant treatment effects may lead to especially severe problems
 here if the assumption is false.
 According to Wolfers (2006, AER), if the treatment effect is not constant
 (e.g., it is short lived, it takes time, it changes slope, etc.), then the
 model is misspecified.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Here are a few additional notes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We can think about the model as overcontrolling since the trends may be
 affected by treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The estimated trend will be a weighted average of the trend observed in
 the pre and post periods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
So, there is no problem if we only have one post-period.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Analagously, there is no problem if we estimate the effect for each post-period
 in a model that allows for dynamic treatment effects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Panel Data Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Fixed Effects Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We estimate fixed effects (FE) models to control for unobserved fixed omitted
 variables.
 The 
\series bold
\emph on
model
\series default
\emph default
 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[y_{it}]=\alpha_{i}+\theta T_{it}+x_{it}^{\prime}\delta+A_{i}^{\prime}\gamma=\alpha_{i}+\theta T_{it}+x_{it}^{\prime}\delta
\]

\end_inset

The main 
\series bold
\emph on
assumption
\series default
\emph default
 is that selection is based only on observable, time-variant characteristics,
 i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}^{\prime}\delta$
\end_inset

, and time-invariant unobservables, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Characteristics with time-invariant effects (notice how 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{i}^{\prime}\gamma$
\end_inset

 has no 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 subscript) can be estimated, but it would be equivalent to estimating deviation
s from means.
 The individual fixed effects capture all time-invariant characteristics
 with time-invariant effects on outcomes, regardless of whether said characteris
tics are observable or unobservable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to estiamte the FE model, we use a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

demeaning approach.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{it} & = & \alpha_{i}+\theta T_{it}+x_{it}^{\prime}\delta+\epsilon_{it}\\
\bar{y}_{i} & = & \alpha_{i}+\theta\bar{T}_{i}+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\delta+\bar{\epsilon}_{i}\\
(y_{it}-\bar{y}_{i}) & = & (T_{it}-\bar{T}_{i})\theta+(x_{it}-\bar{x}_{i})^{\prime}\delta+(\epsilon_{it}-\bar{\epsilon}_{i})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The estimator obtained from an OLS regression of the third equation is called
 the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

within estimator,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fixed effects estimator.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 In contrast, a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

between estimate
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is obtained from a regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{T}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Intuitively, we say that the individual fixed effects, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}$
\end_inset

, are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

absorbed,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

removed,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as opposed to being estimated.
 This way of writing the estimator highlights that individuals serve as
 their own control, i.e., they provide their own counterfactuals.
 It also shows that the FE model exploits the fact that we can obtain a
 consistent estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 without being able to consistently estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Although, it is important to note that this implies we cannot consistently
 estimate the conditional expectation function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Simple Example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we are interested in the effect of sunshine on happiness,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H_{i}=\alpha+S_{i}\beta+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 tells us whether those who experience more sunshine are happier.
 Yet, suppose we are concerned that types of people who live in sunny areas
 are systemically different in ways that relate to happiness.
 So instead, we wish to estimate
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H_{it}=\alpha_{i}+S_{it}\beta+\epsilon_{it}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 tells us whether 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

those who experience more sunshine are happier after adjusting for fixed,
 unobservable characteristics with time-invariant effects.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 To estimate the model, we use a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

demeaning approach,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H_{it}-\bar{H}_{i}=(S_{it}-\bar{S}_{i})\beta+(\epsilon_{it}-\bar{\epsilon}_{i})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 tells us whether 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

individuals are happier than usual when they experience more sunshine than
 usual.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Important to note, selection on time-varying unobservables is a threat!
 In this example, those unobservables would consist of weekends, holidays,
 health, etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Real Example: A Family Fixed Effects Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Ashenfelter and Krueger (1994) considered earnings and education among identical
 twins.
 Their study was meant to address ability bias, so they assumed that ability,
 along with other characteristics related to outcomes, are constant among
 twins.
 The model they considered was
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{if}=\alpha_{f}+T_{if}\theta+\epsilon_{if}
\]

\end_inset

So, within families, how is 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 associated with outcomes? 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, the study was not convincing.
 Some twins get more education than their sibling because of different unobserva
ble characteristics.
 It would have been more convincing if 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

treatment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 would have been outside of the control of the individual.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Carrel and Hoekstra (2011): Treatment is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

number of classmates from homes with domestic violence.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Lindo (2011): Treatment is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

born after a father's job displacement.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Non-linear Models Are Tricky
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With non-linear models, the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

demeaning approach
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 does not work.
 We can use an unconditional maximum likelihood (ML) model, in which we
 include a dummy for each group.
 Though, this method is usually inconsistent because of an incidental parameters
 problem (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is fixed, while 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Logit, on the other hand, is an exception because, like in a linear model,
 the fixed effects can be conditioned.
 Yet, estimating marginal effects requires estimation of all the parameters,
 including fixed effects.
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
ME_{x_{1i}}=\alpha_{1}F^{\prime}(\alpha_{0}+\alpha_{1}x_{1i}+\alpha_{2}x_{2i})
\]

\end_inset


\emph default
where 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is the specified CDF.
 Thus, FE logit cannot give us the exact 
\begin_inset Formula $ME_{x_{1i}}$
\end_inset

, but we get an odds ratio instead.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Measurement Error Bias
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The bias of measurement error tends to be worse for FE models because the
 fixed effects increase the variance of noise relative to the variance of
 signal.
 To elaborate, averages contain relatively lots of signal and little noise
 since the errors cancel out in the average.
 Yet, by removing the average, the noise to signal ratio increases, which
 leads to greater bias.
 This fact is true for different models as well.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
First-Differencing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The first-differencing (FD) approach also eliminates fixed effects, and
 the FD estimator is identical to the FE estimator when 
\begin_inset Formula $T=2$
\end_inset

.
 Though, FD is not as efficient as FE.
 Intuitively, if there is a policy change, this approach identifies using
 two observations.
 Still, one may prefer its identifying assumption:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[\epsilon_{it}-\epsilon_{i,t-1}|x_{it}-x_{i,t-1}]=0
\]

\end_inset

Even so, FD is not very common.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Random Effect Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=\alpha_{i}+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\epsilon_{it}
\]

\end_inset

The FE model treats 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}$
\end_inset

 as parameters to be estimated or removed because of its possible correlation
 with the regressors that would otherwise introduce bias.
 The random effects (RE) model, on the other hand, treats 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}$
\end_inset

 as an i.i.d.
 random variable with mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\alpha}^{2}$
\end_inset

 that is part of a composite error term.
 This means that 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}$
\end_inset

 is assumed to be uncorrelated with the regressors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

So, RE has nothing to do with addressing bias.
 Above all, it is about modeling the error term (GLS), which exhibits serial
 correlation due to the individual component.
 Also, even though both estimators are consistent, the RE estimator is more
 efficient than FE if the exogeneity assumption holds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Formally,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=c_{i}+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[y_{it}|c_{i},x_{it}]=c_{i}+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta
\]

\end_inset

which assumed by both the RE and FE models.
 Continuing on,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[y_{it}|x_{it}]=E[c_{i}|x_{it}]+x_{it}^{\prime}\beta
\]

\end_inset

It is important to note that the RE model assumes that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[c_{i}|x_{it}]=\alpha
\]

\end_inset

but the FE model does not.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Tests of Internal Validity (Diff-in-Diff and FE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Most tests rely on having data long before intervention
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Compare trends in outcome.
 Sometimes we see a formal test in papers, which is weak.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Compare trends in observables
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Any deviations coinciding with treatment is a threat
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Look for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

effects
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of programs before they're introduced.
 This indicates selection into treatment or dissimilar trends, which could
 be a problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Consider treatment effect dynamics.
 Do they make sense?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Look for effects on outcomes in populations where they should not be present.
 This 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

falsification exercise
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

placebo test
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 applies to all research designs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Treatment effect heterogeneity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Interact the treatment variable with the variable of interest instead, e.g.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Treat}\times\mbox{Male}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Restrict the sample to the group of interest.
 With this approach, the parameters can vary, and it is the preferred approach.
 It is known as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

stratfication.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
A Brief Outline of Diff-in-Diff
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Basic Idea
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we randomly assign treatment to some units (or nature assigns treatment
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

as if
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 by random assignment).
 To estimate the treatment effect, we could just compare the treated units
 before and after treatment.
 However, we might pick up the effects of other factors that changed around
 the time of treatment.
 Therefore, we use a control group to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

difference out
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 these confounding factors and isolate the treatment effect.
 Diff-in-diff brings in an untreated comparison group to tell us how outcomes
 would have evolved in the absence of treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Motivation: 
\series default
\emph default
To estimate unbiased causal treatment effects under identifying assumptions
 believed and shown to be reasonable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Identification Assumption:
\series default
\emph default
 Absent the treatment, the difference between the treatment and control
 groups would have remained the same in the post-period as in the pre-period.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The change for the control group provides a good counterfactual for the
 change that would have been observed for the treated group in the absence
 of treatment.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Unlike differences across time, it is permissible if other (observable or
 unobservable) factors lead to changes in outcomes, so long as they similarly
 affect both the treatment and control groups.
 Unlike differences across groups, it is permissible if there are different
 factors between groups related to outocmes similarly pre and post.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Diff-in-Diff: Without Regression
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One approach is to simply take the mean value of each group's outcome before
 and after treatment, and then calculate the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

differences-in-differences
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of the means.
\end_layout

