\begin_layout Subsection*
Diff-in-Diff: With Regression
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the following model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{it}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}\mbox{Treat}_{i}+\beta_{2}\mbox{After}_{t}+\beta_{3}\mbox{Treat}_{i}\times\mbox{After}_{t}+\epsilon_{it}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Treat}_{i}=1$
\end_inset

 if person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is in the treatment group and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{After}_{t}=1$
\end_inset

 if time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is after treatment has occurred.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{3}$
\end_inset

 gives us the Diff-in-Diff estimate of the treatment effect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Tests of Identification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Graph the outcomes for treatment and control groups before and after treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Do they appear to be following the same trajectory pre-treatment?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Show how time-varying controls that are exogenous to the treatment vary
 before and after treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Intuition: If exogenous controls diverge significantly after treatment,
 it makes one worry that the control group was not really a good counterfactual
 for the treatment group.
 Important to note, we are especially worried about diverging changes, not
 different levels
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Examine whether the diff-in-diff estimator changes when you include time-varying
 controls that are exogenous to the treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Intuition: If including exogenous time-varying controls causes the estimate
 to decrease, then we worry that treatment and control groups have diverged
 anyway.
 Above all, if including observables changes the estimate, how can we be
 sure that including unobservables would not change the estimate.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Estimate leading dummy indicators as a placebo test.
 Do the treatment and control groups diverge pre-treatment?
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $y_{it}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}\mbox{Year}_{t}+\beta_{2}\mbox{State}_{i}+\beta_{3}(\mbox{Post}\cdot\mbox{Treat})_{it}+\beta_{4}(\mbox{Year Prior to Treat}\cdot\mbox{Treat})_{it}+\epsilon_{it}$
\end_inset

.
 Hopefully, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{4}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Drop all post treatment data, assume treatment occurred one year earlier,
 two years earlier, etc, and then use the same diff-in-diff research design.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Falsification Test: Use the same research design to show that treatment
 does not affect outcomes it should not affect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Triple-Difference: Add a third difference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Show the effects are larger for subgroups whom you would expect to be more
 affected, ex-ante.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Include state-specific linear time trends: Ask whether deviations from trend
 in the post-period relative to the pre-period are different for the treatment
 and control groups.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Going in this direction implies making the case that treatment is the only
 reason outcomes differ systematically from pre to post.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
